  "body": "https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#tuple\r\n\r\nIn the example:\r\n\r\n> A status is an example tuple consisting of a code (a three-digit number) and text (a byte sequence).\r\n\r\nI would expect \"status\" to be a top-level definition, and \"code\" and \"text\" to be child definitions of \"status\". (I'd also expect byte sequence to link to its definition).\r\n\r\n> Let statusInstance be the status (200, `OK`).\r\n\r\nI'd expect \"status\" to link back to its definition.\r\n\r\n> If statusInstance’s code is 404, then\r\n\r\nI'd expect \"code\" to link back to its definition.\r\n\r\nI'm currently reviewing a PR that uses tuples in this no-linking way & it feels difficult to read and navigate, but infra appears to (accidentally?) recommend this style.\r\n\r\nIs it deliberate that these aren't linked?",
